#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186558900 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1487794f-abfc-57b1-b6ae-51c4d2693ef9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{64}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186558858} {:answer/id #uuid "6782dd8c-c0fc-5d7c-a448-890180571e34" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{64}{15}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186558883} {:answer/id #uuid "c1d48d34-4e58-5532-81af-e55c29c295c9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{256}{15}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186558898} {:answer/id #uuid "c4d66181-9bc7-5851-984e-4c8789977fcd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{128}{15}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186558902} {:answer/id #uuid "e4410879-6b1b-5a62-830e-4d4b7d399fe9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "- \\frac{128}{15}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186558918}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186558902} :answer-field/id #uuid "e54728e9-70eb-5393-a866-3aa8f9ad5712" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186558919}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "c48df844-d520-58e8-ae86-9c673170ff47" :question/problem "Using the change of variables \n\n$$\nu=8 x - 2 y,\\,v=x + y,\n$$\n\ncalculate the area of the finite region $D$ bounded by the curve $(8 x - 2 y)^{2}-(x + y)=0$ and the line $x + y=16.$" :question/retired true :question/worked-solution "The area of the required region is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}dxdy.\n$$\n\nLet's define a transformation $T$ as follows: \n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required area, we will use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}dxdy={∬}_{Δ}|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\text{ }dudv\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$  \n\nNote that the changes of variables\n\n$$\nu=8 x - 2 y,\\,v=x + y\n$$\n\n \n\ngives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}u(x,y) \\\\ v(x,y)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTo compute the required area, we proceed in four steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $D$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$\n\nSubstituting $8 x - 2 y=u$ and $x + y=v$ into the equation of the first curve, we get\n\n$$\n(8 x - 2 y)^{2}-(x + y)=0\\,⟹\\,u^{2}-v=0\\,⟹\\,v=u^{2}.\n$$\n\nwhile substituting $x + y=v$ into the equation of the second curve, we obtain\n\n$$\nv=16.\n$$\n\nTo find the intersections of the curves in the $uv$-plane, we solve the following system:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}v=u^{2} \\\\ v=16\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⟹\\,u=\\pm 4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the intersection points are $(\\pm 4,16).$\n\nTherefore, our domain in the $uv$-plane has the type I representation\n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,-4\\le u\\le 4,\\,u^{2}\\le v\\le 16\\}.\n$$\n\n![](images/38/387e22e062c4ff7f24d26561fe369041f11f59a2b0305f8dc7b3ce49d411031a.png)\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x}&\\frac{∂u}{∂y}\\\\\\frac{∂v}{∂x}&\\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\ &=\\begin{vmatrix}8&-2\\\\1&1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\ &=10\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}={\\left(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)}\\right)}^{-1}=(10)^{-1}=\\frac{1}{10}.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$  In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 4**: Compute the required area.\n\nWe calculate the required area by performing the change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\iint_D dxdy &=\\iint_Δ |\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\,dudv \\\\ &=\\iint_Δ |\\frac{1}{10}|\\,dudv \\\\ &=\\frac{1}{10}\\int_{-4}^{4}[\\int_{u^{2}}^{16}dv]du \\\\ &=\\frac{1}{10}\\int_{-4}^{4}[v]_{u^{2}}^{16}du \\\\ &=\\frac{1}{10}\\int_{-4}^{4}(16)-(u^{2})du \\\\ &=\\frac{1}{10}\\int_{-4}^{4}(16 - u^{2})du \\\\ &=\\frac{1}{10}[- \\frac{u^{3}}{3} + 16 u]_{-4}^{4} \\\\ &=\\frac{1}{10}(((64)+\\left(- \\frac{64}{3}\\right))-((-64)+\\left(\\frac{64}{3}\\right))) \\\\ &=\\frac{1}{10}\\left(\\frac{256}{3}\\right) \\\\ &=\\frac{128}{15}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186558904 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0c128895-028a-5333-bd5a-8ee667b07a6f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{21}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186558854} {:answer/id #uuid "ca8ec194-90f6-583a-9a4b-6db556579326" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186558906} {:answer/id #uuid "dc16d887-5db7-5718-ab28-a3f95ddc848b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "- \\frac{4}{21}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186558912} {:answer/id #uuid "debaf4ed-2222-5306-b87c-ad969bf33fa5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{8}{21}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186558913} {:answer/id #uuid "e7dbec56-34b1-53a9-a22b-aba486b38599" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{21}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186558920}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186558920} :answer-field/id #uuid "dadd3d78-081c-5068-a70c-3f770eafd54e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186558911}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "c7168dbd-f88c-576f-98c9-c412c7458ef0" :question/problem "Using the change of variables \n\n$$\nu=5 x - 2 y,\\,v=x + y,\n$$\n\ncalculate the area of the finite region $D$ bounded by the curve $(5 x - 2 y)^{2}-(x + y)=0$ and the line $x + y=1.$" :question/retired true :question/worked-solution "The area of the required region is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}dxdy.\n$$\n\nLet's define a transformation $T$ as follows: \n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required area, we will use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}dxdy={∬}_{Δ}|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\text{ }dudv\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$  \n\nNote that the changes of variables\n\n$$\nu=5 x - 2 y,\\,v=x + y\n$$\n\n \n\ngives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}u(x,y) \\\\ v(x,y)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTo compute the required area, we proceed in four steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $D$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$\n\nSubstituting $5 x - 2 y=u$ and $x + y=v$ into the equation of the first curve, we get\n\n$$\n(5 x - 2 y)^{2}-(x + y)=0\\,⟹\\,u^{2}-v=0\\,⟹\\,v=u^{2}.\n$$\n\nwhile substituting $x + y=v$ into the equation of the second curve, we obtain\n\n$$\nv=1.\n$$\n\nTo find the intersections of the curves in the $uv$-plane, we solve the following system:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}v=u^{2} \\\\ v=1\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⟹\\,u=\\pm 1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the intersection points are $(\\pm 1,1).$\n\nTherefore, our domain in the $uv$-plane has the type I representation\n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,-1\\le u\\le 1,\\,u^{2}\\le v\\le 1\\}.\n$$\n\n![](images/39/39d20dd045c97cae079f3839b4db071bd6b3b9ff133299ed4ae4d9421a55fee8.png)\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x}&\\frac{∂u}{∂y}\\\\\\frac{∂v}{∂x}&\\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\ &=\\begin{vmatrix}5&-2\\\\1&1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\ &=7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}={\\left(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)}\\right)}^{-1}=(7)^{-1}=\\frac{1}{7}.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$  In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 4**: Compute the required area.\n\nWe calculate the required area by performing the change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\iint_D dxdy &=\\iint_Δ |\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\,dudv \\\\ &=\\iint_Δ |\\frac{1}{7}|\\,dudv \\\\ &=\\frac{1}{7}\\int_{-1}^{1}[\\int_{u^{2}}^{1}dv]du \\\\ &=\\frac{1}{7}\\int_{-1}^{1}[v]_{u^{2}}^{1}du \\\\ &=\\frac{1}{7}\\int_{-1}^{1}(1)-(u^{2})du \\\\ &=\\frac{1}{7}\\int_{-1}^{1}(1 - u^{2})du \\\\ &=\\frac{1}{7}[- \\frac{u^{3}}{3} + u]_{-1}^{1} \\\\ &=\\frac{1}{7}(((1)+\\left(- \\frac{1}{3}\\right))-((-1)+\\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right))) \\\\ &=\\frac{1}{7}\\left(\\frac{4}{3}\\right) \\\\ &=\\frac{4}{21}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186558917 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3e60f5e8-67d9-5947-9c0b-02d3e2848b7c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{18}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186558874} {:answer/id #uuid "623d947d-bfd7-5e1b-be6c-685935878d3f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{36}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186558880} {:answer/id #uuid "838009ad-af94-5c1e-9dc6-233cad98d542" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{9}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186558885} {:answer/id #uuid "d1051dd5-4fb6-5657-bf68-8d1d9e1a8de1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "- \\frac{18}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186558908} {:answer/id #uuid "f7d2af79-5742-5740-a01d-6a65765b9184" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{27}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186558924}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186558874} :answer-field/id #uuid "24456fc9-3202-52e6-96c1-45748de19c53" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186558863}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "e0921561-14dc-5c7d-b935-87b9062309fa" :question/problem "Using the change of variables \n\n$$\nu=8 x - 2 y,\\,v=x + y,\n$$\n\ncalculate the area of the finite region $D$ bounded by the curve $(8 x - 2 y)^{2}-(x + y)=0$ and the line $x + y=9.$" :question/retired true :question/worked-solution "The area of the required region is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}dxdy.\n$$\n\nLet's define a transformation $T$ as follows: \n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required area, we will use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}dxdy={∬}_{Δ}|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\text{ }dudv\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$  \n\nNote that the changes of variables\n\n$$\nu=8 x - 2 y,\\,v=x + y\n$$\n\n \n\ngives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}u(x,y) \\\\ v(x,y)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTo compute the required area, we proceed in four steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $D$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$\n\nSubstituting $8 x - 2 y=u$ and $x + y=v$ into the equation of the first curve, we get\n\n$$\n(8 x - 2 y)^{2}-(x + y)=0\\,⟹\\,u^{2}-v=0\\,⟹\\,v=u^{2}.\n$$\n\nwhile substituting $x + y=v$ into the equation of the second curve, we obtain\n\n$$\nv=9.\n$$\n\nTo find the intersections of the curves in the $uv$-plane, we solve the following system:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}v=u^{2} \\\\ v=9\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⟹\\,u=\\pm 3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the intersection points are $(\\pm 3,9).$\n\nTherefore, our domain in the $uv$-plane has the type I representation\n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,-3\\le u\\le 3,\\,u^{2}\\le v\\le 9\\}.\n$$\n\n![](images/18/18c4c61a55dd2a361b82a208eb9a0d26f3fcfa213fe83b05f23644c9f79becaa.png)\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x}&\\frac{∂u}{∂y}\\\\\\frac{∂v}{∂x}&\\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\ &=\\begin{vmatrix}8&-2\\\\1&1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\ &=10\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}={\\left(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)}\\right)}^{-1}=(10)^{-1}=\\frac{1}{10}.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$  In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 4**: Compute the required area.\n\nWe calculate the required area by performing the change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\iint_D dxdy &=\\iint_Δ |\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\,dudv \\\\ &=\\iint_Δ |\\frac{1}{10}|\\,dudv \\\\ &=\\frac{1}{10}\\int_{-3}^{3}[\\int_{u^{2}}^{9}dv]du \\\\ &=\\frac{1}{10}\\int_{-3}^{3}[v]_{u^{2}}^{9}du \\\\ &=\\frac{1}{10}\\int_{-3}^{3}(9)-(u^{2})du \\\\ &=\\frac{1}{10}\\int_{-3}^{3}(9 - u^{2})du \\\\ &=\\frac{1}{10}[- \\frac{u^{3}}{3} + 9 u]_{-3}^{3} \\\\ &=\\frac{1}{10}(((27)+(-9))-((-27)+(9))) \\\\ &=\\frac{1}{10}(36) \\\\ &=\\frac{18}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186558926 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "067912ba-31ed-5eca-bf1b-2e1a722add95" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "32 \\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186558853} {:answer/id #uuid "5dd8c0ca-63f1-573c-9fc6-3d6c2d297b5f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "16 \\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186558879} {:answer/id #uuid "80b482c2-0595-595b-b947-9b4fc50000dd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "- 32 \\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186558884} {:answer/id #uuid "865bc14e-79fe-5274-a9ab-657177e6cb94" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "48 \\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186558886} {:answer/id #uuid "9ebee877-3b12-5791-b4d8-5dd7a7a5887b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "64 \\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186558893}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186558853} :answer-field/id #uuid "3313f71a-a789-5b76-a642-c2288a3b5693" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186558870}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "fcb07a25-db40-5466-b856-2310cb940e0e" :question/problem "Using the change of variables \n\n$$\nu=x - 4 y,\\,v=y,\n$$\n\ncalculate the area of the finite region $D$ bounded by the curve $(x - 4 y)^{2}-(y)=4$ and the line $y=8.$" :question/retired true :question/worked-solution "The area of the required region is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}dxdy.\n$$\n\nLet's define a transformation $T$ as follows: \n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required area, we will use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}dxdy={∬}_{Δ}|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\text{ }dudv\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$  \n\nNote that the changes of variables\n\n$$\nu=x - 4 y,\\,v=y\n$$\n\n \n\ngives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}u(x,y) \\\\ v(x,y)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTo compute the required area, we proceed in four steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $D$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$\n\nSubstituting $x - 4 y=u$ and $y=v$ into the equation of the first curve, we get\n\n$$\n(x - 4 y)^{2}-(y)=4\\,⟹\\,u^{2}-v=4\\,⟹\\,v=u^{2} - 4.\n$$\n\nwhile substituting $y=v$ into the equation of the second curve, we obtain\n\n$$\nv=8.\n$$\n\nTo find the intersections of the curves in the $uv$-plane, we solve the following system:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}v=u^{2} - 4 \\\\ v=8\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⟹\\,u=\\pm 2 \\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the intersection points are $(\\pm 2 \\sqrt{3},8).$\n\nTherefore, our domain in the $uv$-plane has the type I representation\n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,- 2 \\sqrt{3}\\le u\\le 2 \\sqrt{3},\\,u^{2} - 4\\le v\\le 8\\}.\n$$\n\n![](images/23/2369c3299b61f996c6203e1e45a7a62c74cf9e8f4691c2d0941bdeec0a5ae53c.png)\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x}&\\frac{∂u}{∂y}\\\\\\frac{∂v}{∂x}&\\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\ &=\\begin{vmatrix}1&-4\\\\0&1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\ &=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}={\\left(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)}\\right)}^{-1}=(1)^{-1}=1.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$  In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 4**: Compute the required area.\n\nWe calculate the required area by performing the change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\iint_D dxdy &=\\iint_Δ |\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\,dudv \\\\ &=\\iint_Δ |1|\\,dudv \\\\ &=1\\int_{- 2 \\sqrt{3}}^{2 \\sqrt{3}}[\\int_{u^{2} - 4}^{8}dv]du \\\\ &=1\\int_{- 2 \\sqrt{3}}^{2 \\sqrt{3}}[v]_{u^{2} - 4}^{8}du \\\\ &=1\\int_{- 2 \\sqrt{3}}^{2 \\sqrt{3}}(8)-(u^{2} - 4)du \\\\ &=1\\int_{- 2 \\sqrt{3}}^{2 \\sqrt{3}}(12 - u^{2})du \\\\ &=1[- \\frac{u^{3}}{3} + 12 u]_{- 2 \\sqrt{3}}^{2 \\sqrt{3}} \\\\ &=1(((24 \\sqrt{3})+(- 8 \\sqrt{3}))-((- 24 \\sqrt{3})+(8 \\sqrt{3}))) \\\\ &=1(32 \\sqrt{3}) \\\\ &=32 \\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186560226 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "567b3d3d-d78d-511c-9b35-cd452b837745" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(-3,1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186560231} {:answer/id #uuid "69fb122d-4d8d-5d0c-8fdf-0488493b4db2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(-3,0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186560233} {:answer/id #uuid "6efa1da8-2732-5f16-8aed-cc275fa27b86" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(-5,-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186560235} {:answer/id #uuid "94015238-849a-544e-926f-f33e35cb5954" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(-4,-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186560241} {:answer/id #uuid "f19d2da3-09a1-5877-b2ce-524aeb8ace2e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(-3,-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186560251}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186560251} :answer-field/id #uuid "32a3ba95-14d4-5176-8a6e-171c8d545d55" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186560225}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "386b29aa-4458-560c-9279-5b720c1afe27" :question/problem "Given that $f(x,y)= (y + 1)\\ln (x + 4),$ find the points $(x, y)$ where $\\nabla f(x,y) = \\mathbf 0.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the partial derivatives: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}((y + 1)\\ln (x + 4)) \\\\  & =\\frac{y + 1}{x + 4} \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}((y + 1)\\ln (x + 4)) \\\\  & =\\ln(x + 4)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\nabla f(x,y) = \\dfrac {y + 1}{x + 4} \\, \\mathbf i + \\ln(x + 4) \\, \\mathbf j.\n$$\n\nNow, we solve $\\nabla f(x,y) = \\mathbf 0\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\dfrac {y + 1}{x + 4} \\, \\mathbf i + \\ln(x + 4) \\, \\mathbf j = 0 \\mathbf i + 0\\mathbf j\n$$\n\nBy equating the coefficients of $\\mathbf{i}$ on both sides of the equation (and similarly with $\\mathbf{j}$), we obtain\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{y + 1}{x + 4}=0 \\\\ \\ln(x + 4)=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the first equation, we get $y + 1=0,$ which means $y=-1.$ From the second equation, we obtain $\\ln(x + 4)=0,$ which means that $x=-3.$\n\nTherefore, $\\nabla f(x,y) =\\mathbf 0$ at the point $(-3,-1).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186560229 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0b2e65d6-54bc-57a6-a65a-140826e6ecb3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(-2,3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186560221} {:answer/id #uuid "5458a70e-13a8-579e-84a4-8d6dc6f7c8b3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(-1,3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186560230} {:answer/id #uuid "799960aa-dd71-5a7b-bb02-5a3d4913b2f2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(-1,-3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186560236} {:answer/id #uuid "8958eb41-d02e-5748-952d-1ecd69cf0652" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(-3,3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186560240} {:answer/id #uuid "9b6e7e1c-e225-574d-b9da-14f9feea22d7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(-1,0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186560243}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186560230} :answer-field/id #uuid "3a2cba62-d6af-5479-baaa-023b4d675d28" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186560227}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "427b5a56-3af0-5b6f-a52d-5b35601fc9cc" :question/problem "Given that $f(x,y)= (y - 3)\\ln (x + 2),$ find the points $(x, y)$ where $\\nabla f(x,y) = \\mathbf 0.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the partial derivatives: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}((y - 3)\\ln (x + 2)) \\\\  & =\\frac{y - 3}{x + 2} \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}((y - 3)\\ln (x + 2)) \\\\  & =\\ln(x + 2)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\nabla f(x,y) = \\dfrac {y - 3}{x + 2} \\, \\mathbf i + \\ln(x + 2) \\, \\mathbf j.\n$$\n\nNow, we solve $\\nabla f(x,y) = \\mathbf 0\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\dfrac {y - 3}{x + 2} \\, \\mathbf i + \\ln(x + 2) \\, \\mathbf j = 0 \\mathbf i + 0\\mathbf j\n$$\n\nBy equating the coefficients of $\\mathbf{i}$ on both sides of the equation (and similarly with $\\mathbf{j}$), we obtain\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{y - 3}{x + 2}=0 \\\\ \\ln(x + 2)=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the first equation, we get $y - 3=0,$ which means $y=3.$ From the second equation, we obtain $\\ln(x + 2)=0,$ which means that $x=-1.$\n\nTherefore, $\\nabla f(x,y) =\\mathbf 0$ at the point $(-1,3).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186560248 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "59449bac-6962-5a16-a33c-e8613d668c3d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(1,-3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186560232} {:answer/id #uuid "6a3127d6-0562-5c26-9acf-f7f09b3aa64a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(3,3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186560234} {:answer/id #uuid "9acc15e0-cf77-5237-8bba-0f9d27d1e5c1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(2,-3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186560242} {:answer/id #uuid "9f154bdd-b5bc-57de-adcc-52409facc55f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(3,-3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186560244} {:answer/id #uuid "b36b1aa0-5151-5195-9eea-63f556cbf232" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(3,0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186560247}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186560244} :answer-field/id #uuid "f1363740-4bad-5cae-9277-fec03ba7da65" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186560250}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "b8b31e81-79d2-50e9-aa69-dea3d4c28a69" :question/problem "Given that $f(x,y)= (y + 3)\\ln (x - 2),$ find the points $(x, y)$ where $\\nabla f(x,y) = \\mathbf 0.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the partial derivatives: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}((y + 3)\\ln (x - 2)) \\\\  & =\\frac{y + 3}{x - 2} \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}((y + 3)\\ln (x - 2)) \\\\  & =\\ln(x - 2)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\nabla f(x,y) = \\dfrac {y + 3}{x - 2} \\, \\mathbf i + \\ln(x - 2) \\, \\mathbf j.\n$$\n\nNow, we solve $\\nabla f(x,y) = \\mathbf 0\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\dfrac {y + 3}{x - 2} \\, \\mathbf i + \\ln(x - 2) \\, \\mathbf j = 0 \\mathbf i + 0\\mathbf j\n$$\n\nBy equating the coefficients of $\\mathbf{i}$ on both sides of the equation (and similarly with $\\mathbf{j}$), we obtain\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{y + 3}{x - 2}=0 \\\\ \\ln(x - 2)=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the first equation, we get $y + 3=0,$ which means $y=-3.$ From the second equation, we obtain $\\ln(x - 2)=0,$ which means that $x=3.$\n\nTherefore, $\\nabla f(x,y) =\\mathbf 0$ at the point $(3,-3).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186560249 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3d754153-fc36-5a9f-8e3a-712811db4818" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(-1,0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186560228} {:answer/id #uuid "7a04eb94-3e8f-5e34-a445-2669ab21c905" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(-1,1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186560237} {:answer/id #uuid "7ce4e93e-ebf7-5551-ab02-5b20e6f42b2b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(-1,-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186560238} {:answer/id #uuid "afe9dc9d-2224-5c05-9bd1-ca3051880b20" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(-3,1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186560246} {:answer/id #uuid "fb5daf4f-98be-5821-929f-7d6cc71f5edd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(-2,1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186560255}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186560237} :answer-field/id #uuid "1c943c87-8a4d-5a5b-b6cb-7302a54e2cba" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186560223}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "d1ec7812-3978-5a02-ac42-759b550f6d05" :question/problem "Given that $f(x,y)= (y - 1)\\ln (x + 2),$ find the points $(x, y)$ where $\\nabla f(x,y) = \\mathbf 0.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the partial derivatives: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}((y - 1)\\ln (x + 2)) \\\\  & =\\frac{y - 1}{x + 2} \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}((y - 1)\\ln (x + 2)) \\\\  & =\\ln(x + 2)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\nabla f(x,y) = \\dfrac {y - 1}{x + 2} \\, \\mathbf i + \\ln(x + 2) \\, \\mathbf j.\n$$\n\nNow, we solve $\\nabla f(x,y) = \\mathbf 0\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\dfrac {y - 1}{x + 2} \\, \\mathbf i + \\ln(x + 2) \\, \\mathbf j = 0 \\mathbf i + 0\\mathbf j\n$$\n\nBy equating the coefficients of $\\mathbf{i}$ on both sides of the equation (and similarly with $\\mathbf{j}$), we obtain\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{y - 1}{x + 2}=0 \\\\ \\ln(x + 2)=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the first equation, we get $y - 1=0,$ which means $y=1.$ From the second equation, we obtain $\\ln(x + 2)=0,$ which means that $x=-1.$\n\nTherefore, $\\nabla f(x,y) =\\mathbf 0$ at the point $(-1,1).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186560254 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0d088e76-e837-5cbf-b376-1745a403ffe0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(-3,0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186560222} {:answer/id #uuid "1f52df86-7c68-5ef2-ab8a-b6ffb44b99a4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(-4,3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186560224} {:answer/id #uuid "a955bb89-4eee-5ba8-b0f3-4339ceeebe1a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(-5,3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186560245} {:answer/id #uuid "f2226035-4bfc-5f1c-a8eb-16a3f1bc6087" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(-3,3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186560252} {:answer/id #uuid "f28de173-e319-5f89-aad9-dec2d4913e48" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(-3,-3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186560253}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186560252} :answer-field/id #uuid "82083121-5ba5-51cb-a7b7-d2af46422080" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186560239}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "f2b9afa1-6b98-567c-ab1e-6eccf862de16" :question/problem "Given that $f(x,y)= (y - 3)\\ln (x + 4),$ find the points $(x, y)$ where $\\nabla f(x,y) = \\mathbf 0.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the partial derivatives: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}((y - 3)\\ln (x + 4)) \\\\  & =\\frac{y - 3}{x + 4} \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}((y - 3)\\ln (x + 4)) \\\\  & =\\ln(x + 4)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\nabla f(x,y) = \\dfrac {y - 3}{x + 4} \\, \\mathbf i + \\ln(x + 4) \\, \\mathbf j.\n$$\n\nNow, we solve $\\nabla f(x,y) = \\mathbf 0\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\dfrac {y - 3}{x + 4} \\, \\mathbf i + \\ln(x + 4) \\, \\mathbf j = 0 \\mathbf i + 0\\mathbf j\n$$\n\nBy equating the coefficients of $\\mathbf{i}$ on both sides of the equation (and similarly with $\\mathbf{j}$), we obtain\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{y - 3}{x + 4}=0 \\\\ \\ln(x + 4)=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the first equation, we get $y - 3=0,$ which means $y=3.$ From the second equation, we obtain $\\ln(x + 4)=0,$ which means that $x=-3.$\n\nTherefore, $\\nabla f(x,y) =\\mathbf 0$ at the point $(-3,3).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186560308 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "81a4a4d4-c10a-55dc-b43b-8853d6f328f1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "128 u^{6} v^{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186560329} {:answer/id #uuid "8459c7ad-2f1c-54bb-b579-d98e4986a65b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "16 \\sqrt{2} u^{5} v^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186560330} {:answer/id #uuid "86ac4b28-467b-52f4-b7a3-2b20dd726a36" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "16 \\sqrt{2} u^{5} v^{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186560331} {:answer/id #uuid "99db7d55-0ba4-574a-abd0-fad22dde97d8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4 u^{4} v^{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186560332} {:answer/id #uuid "de54362b-6031-500a-b509-e99fd59af211" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "16 \\sqrt{2} u^{4} v^{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186560345}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186560331} :answer-field/id #uuid "24f470c7-3e68-5664-8116-49ea2082cafa" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186560312}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "1d9ba2e0-102f-5ae7-abb7-4c8e12292bfc" :question/problem "$$\n{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}\\boxed{}\\,\\text{d}A\n$$\n\nConsider the surface integral of the function \n$f(x,y,z)=x^{2} y^{2} z^{2}$ \nover the parametric surface \n\n$$\nr(u,v)=⟨2 u^{2},\\,v,\\,v⟩\n$$\n\nfor $(u,v)\\in D⊆{R}^{2}.$ Assume $u\\geq0.$ What is the missing expression? Choose an integrand equal to the required expression as a function on $D$.\n\n*Hint: You can use the fact that $‖{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}‖=4 \\sqrt{2} u.$*" :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ with parametrization $r(u,v)$ for $(u,v)\\in D,$ the surface integral of a scalar function $f$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}f(r(u,v))‖{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}‖\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nNotice that we are told that the norm of the fundamental vector product for our surface is\n\n$$\n‖{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}‖=4 \\sqrt{2} u.\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the coordinate expressions of $r(u,v)$ into $f(x,y,z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(u,v)) & =(x^{2} y^{2} z^{2})\\,{|}_{x=2 u^{2}\\,y=v\\,z=v} \\\\  & =(2 u^{2}\\cdot 2 u^{2})\\cdot v^{2}\\cdot v^{2} \\\\  & =4 u^{4} v^{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be written as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}f(r(u,v))‖{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}‖\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\,4 \\sqrt{2} u 4 u^{4} v^{4}\\,\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186560315 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "030d3593-551c-5780-836c-e6aa7edd6559" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\left(- 4 u^{2} + 1 \\left(u + v\\right) v^{2}\\right) \\left(8 v^{2} + 1\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186560300} {:answer/id #uuid "1c567497-6459-5620-830a-356a99490e90" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\left(- 4 u^{2} + 1 \\left(u + v\\right) v^{2}\\right) \\sqrt{8 v^{2} + 1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186560307} {:answer/id #uuid "33bb8fed-592d-5e3a-8930-5d28957eefc1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\sqrt{8 v^{2} + 1} \\left(- 4 u^{2} - v^{3} + v^{2} \\left(u + v\\right)\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186560317} {:answer/id #uuid "34bd55fd-9014-5837-a3ed-30210221cafa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\sqrt{8 v^{2} + 1} \\left(- 4 u^{2} - 2 v^{3} + v^{2} \\left(u + v\\right)\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186560318} {:answer/id #uuid "da65ba3f-7987-5418-bed4-7007a24d71df" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "- 4 u^{2} + 1 \\left(u + v\\right) v^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186560342}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186560307} :answer-field/id #uuid "11fa6424-029e-5a79-821e-cb2a038e5fda" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186560305}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "2c8c277a-d40b-52fe-8295-ceb48a2d895c" :question/problem "$$\n{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}\\boxed{}\\,\\text{d}A\n$$\n\nConsider the surface integral of the function \n$f(x,y,z)=- 4 x^{2} + y z$ \nover the parametric surface \n\n$$\nr(u,v)=⟨u,\\,u + v,\\,v^{2}⟩\n$$\n\nfor $(u,v)\\in D⊆{R}^{2}.$ What is the missing expression? Choose an integrand equal to the required expression as a function on $D$.\n\n*Hint: You can use the fact that $‖{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}‖=\\sqrt{8 v^{2} + 1}.$*" :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ with parametrization $r(u,v)$ for $(u,v)\\in D,$ the surface integral of a scalar function $f$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}f(r(u,v))‖{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}‖\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nNotice that we are told that the norm of the fundamental vector product for our surface is\n\n$$\n‖{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}‖=\\sqrt{8 v^{2} + 1}.\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the coordinate expressions of $r(u,v)$ into $f(x,y,z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(u,v)) & =(- 4 x^{2} + y z)\\,{|}_{x=u\\,y=u + v\\,z=v^{2}} \\\\  & =-4\\cdot (u\\cdot u)+(u+v)\\cdot v^{2} \\\\  & =- 4 u^{2} + 1 \\left(u + v\\right) v^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be written as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}f(r(u,v))‖{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}‖\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\,\\left(- 4 u^{2} + 1 \\left(u + v\\right) v^{2}\\right) \\sqrt{8 v^{2} + 1}\\,\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186560320 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "058e5939-7dca-5207-a9cf-7578532c3f28" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\left(18 u^{3} + 18 u^{2} v^{2} + 6 u v\\right) \\sqrt{52 u^{2} + 64 u v^{2} + 64 v^{4} + 144 v^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186560302} {:answer/id #uuid "11a957d3-49ef-56db-ab0f-c02857167b9b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\left(18 u^{2} v^{2} + 6 u v\\right) \\sqrt{52 u^{2} + 64 u v^{2} + 64 v^{4} + 144 v^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186560304} {:answer/id #uuid "1ef0898a-8678-542f-ad45-70587f8bbd8a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\left(- 18 u^{3} + 18 u^{2} v^{2} + 6 u v\\right) \\left(52 u^{2} + 64 u v^{2} + 64 v^{4} + 144 v^{2}\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186560309} {:answer/id #uuid "2b5c61b9-0f4d-5229-83c2-28dbe2298a06" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "- 18 u^{3} + 18 u^{2} v^{2} + 6 u v" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186560314} {:answer/id #uuid "9f011f25-a217-5291-b94d-c478fcc9478a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\left(- 18 u^{3} + 18 u^{2} v^{2} + 6 u v\\right) \\sqrt{52 u^{2} + 64 u v^{2} + 64 v^{4} + 144 v^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186560337}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186560337} :answer-field/id #uuid "db2fc28a-c600-58d0-b8a2-e9ebeb14d152" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186560343}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "3b4808cc-728c-55b7-90f8-734ae2943c4e" :question/problem "$$\n{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}\\boxed{}\\,\\text{d}A\n$$\n\nConsider the surface integral of the function \n$f(x,y,z)=3 x - y^{2} z$ \nover the parametric surface \n\n$$\nr(u,v)=⟨2 u v,\\,- 3 u,\\,2 u - 2 v^{2}⟩\n$$\n\nfor $(u,v)\\in D⊆{R}^{2}.$ What is the missing expression? Choose an integrand equal to the required expression as a function on $D$." :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ with parametrization $r(u,v)$ for $(u,v)\\in D,$ the surface integral of a scalar function $f$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}f(r(u,v))‖{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}‖\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWe first compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{u}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂u},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂u},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂u}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂u}(2 u v),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂u}(- 3 u),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂u}(2 u - 2 v^{2})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2 v,\\,-3,\\,2⟩ \\\\ {r}_{v}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂v},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂v}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂v}(2 u v),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂v}(- 3 u),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂v}(2 u - 2 v^{2})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2 u,\\,0,\\,- 4 v⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′} & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 2 v & -3 & 2 \\\\ 2 u & 0 & - 4 v\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =⟨\\begin{vmatrix}-3 & 2 \\\\ 0 & - 4 v\\end{vmatrix},\\,-\\begin{vmatrix}2 v & 2 \\\\ 2 u & - 4 v\\end{vmatrix},\\,\\begin{vmatrix}2 v & -3 \\\\ 2 u & 0\\end{vmatrix}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨12 v,\\,4 u + 8 v^{2},\\,6 u⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}‖ & =‖⟨12 v,\\,4 u + 8 v^{2},\\,6 u⟩‖ \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(12 v)^{2}+(4 u + 8 v^{2})^{2}+(6 u)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{52 u^{2} + 64 u v^{2} + 64 v^{4} + 144 v^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the coordinate expressions of $r(u,v)$ into $f(x,y,z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(u,v)) & =(3 x - y^{2} z)\\,{|}_{x=2 u v\\,y=- 3 u\\,z=2 u - 2 v^{2}} \\\\  & =6 u v-1\\cdot (- 3 u)^{2}\\cdot (2 u - 2 v^{2}) \\\\  & =- 18 u^{3} + 18 u^{2} v^{2} + 6 u v\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be written as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}f(r(u,v))‖{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}‖\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\,\\left(- 18 u^{3} + 18 u^{2} v^{2} + 6 u v\\right) \\sqrt{52 u^{2} + 64 u v^{2} + 64 v^{4} + 144 v^{2}}\\,\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186560324 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "03e2969c-7bc4-5828-b311-0b75c71bdde5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\left(- 81 u^{3} + 54 u^{2} v^{2} + u v\\right) \\sqrt{18 u^{2} + 24 u v^{2} + 16 v^{4} + 144 v^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186560301} {:answer/id #uuid "473df280-9c01-54be-9ab5-5f9b35d7d479" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\left(54 u^{2} v^{2} + u v\\right) \\sqrt{18 u^{2} + 24 u v^{2} + 16 v^{4} + 144 v^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186560322} {:answer/id #uuid "6f08a77f-a760-599a-8653-c3042e189d26" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "- 81 u^{3} + 54 u^{2} v^{2} + u v" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186560325} {:answer/id #uuid "a07bf10e-5daf-5f77-8a19-1137c0d51608" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\left(- 81 u^{3} + 54 u^{2} v^{2} + u v\\right) \\left(18 u^{2} + 24 u v^{2} + 16 v^{4} + 144 v^{2}\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186560338} {:answer/id #uuid "db499b45-10a8-52f9-b982-757feb90ff5d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\left(81 u^{3} + 54 u^{2} v^{2} + u v\\right) \\sqrt{18 u^{2} + 24 u v^{2} + 16 v^{4} + 144 v^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186560344}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186560301} :answer-field/id #uuid "4deea122-0bc4-5c62-869f-4a630e0ddb1c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186560323}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "69ca8999-9b1e-5bfc-ab7f-c561489f836e" :question/problem "$$\n{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}\\boxed{}\\,\\text{d}A\n$$\n\nConsider the surface integral of the function \n$f(x,y,z)=x - 3 y^{2} z$ \nover the parametric surface \n\n$$\nr(u,v)=⟨u v,\\,- 3 u,\\,3 u - 2 v^{2}⟩\n$$\n\nfor $(u,v)\\in D⊆{R}^{2}.$ What is the missing expression? Choose an integrand equal to the required expression as a function on $D$." :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ with parametrization $r(u,v)$ for $(u,v)\\in D,$ the surface integral of a scalar function $f$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}f(r(u,v))‖{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}‖\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWe first compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{u}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂u},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂u},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂u}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂u}(u v),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂u}(- 3 u),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂u}(3 u - 2 v^{2})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨v,\\,-3,\\,3⟩ \\\\ {r}_{v}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂v},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂v}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂v}(u v),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂v}(- 3 u),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂v}(3 u - 2 v^{2})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨u,\\,0,\\,- 4 v⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′} & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ v & -3 & 3 \\\\ u & 0 & - 4 v\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =⟨\\begin{vmatrix}-3 & 3 \\\\ 0 & - 4 v\\end{vmatrix},\\,-\\begin{vmatrix}v & 3 \\\\ u & - 4 v\\end{vmatrix},\\,\\begin{vmatrix}v & -3 \\\\ u & 0\\end{vmatrix}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨12 v,\\,3 u + 4 v^{2},\\,3 u⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}‖ & =‖⟨12 v,\\,3 u + 4 v^{2},\\,3 u⟩‖ \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(12 v)^{2}+(3 u + 4 v^{2})^{2}+(3 u)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{18 u^{2} + 24 u v^{2} + 16 v^{4} + 144 v^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the coordinate expressions of $r(u,v)$ into $f(x,y,z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(u,v)) & =(x - 3 y^{2} z)\\,{|}_{x=u v\\,y=- 3 u\\,z=3 u - 2 v^{2}} \\\\  & =u v-3\\cdot (- 3 u)^{2}\\cdot (3 u - 2 v^{2}) \\\\  & =- 81 u^{3} + 54 u^{2} v^{2} + u v\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be written as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}f(r(u,v))‖{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}‖\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\,\\left(- 81 u^{3} + 54 u^{2} v^{2} + u v\\right) \\sqrt{18 u^{2} + 24 u v^{2} + 16 v^{4} + 144 v^{2}}\\,\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186560328 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "13b418aa-8f50-529b-a254-a52c443915a0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\left(3 u^{3} + u^{2} v^{2} + 2 u v\\right) \\sqrt{40 u^{2} + 48 u v^{2} + 16 v^{4} + 4 v^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186560306} {:answer/id #uuid "9c93004a-cd83-5a5e-91b3-6afd517b9553" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\left(- 3 u^{3} + u^{2} v^{2} + 2 u v\\right) \\left(40 u^{2} + 48 u v^{2} + 16 v^{4} + 4 v^{2}\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186560334} {:answer/id #uuid "bf0da7f6-74c9-595e-935f-fa2d5e17164f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\left(- 3 u^{3} + u^{2} v^{2} + 2 u v\\right) \\sqrt{40 u^{2} + 48 u v^{2} + 16 v^{4} + 4 v^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186560339} {:answer/id #uuid "d3279af3-c719-5f48-8397-3cee94b39b27" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\left(u^{2} v^{2} + 2 u v\\right) \\sqrt{40 u^{2} + 48 u v^{2} + 16 v^{4} + 4 v^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186560341} {:answer/id #uuid "fdb763ec-517c-56ab-a78d-f970a030bcd1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "- 3 u^{3} + u^{2} v^{2} + 2 u v" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186560347}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186560339} :answer-field/id #uuid "27483cd1-fc63-57b7-82c5-fd8540038450" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186560313}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "7d206b38-df94-5588-845c-04813c06e146" :question/problem "$$\n{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}\\boxed{}\\,\\text{d}A\n$$\n\nConsider the surface integral of the function \n$f(x,y,z)=x - y^{2} z$ \nover the parametric surface \n\n$$\nr(u,v)=⟨2 u v,\\,- u,\\,3 u - v^{2}⟩\n$$\n\nfor $(u,v)\\in D⊆{R}^{2}.$ What is the missing expression? Choose an integrand equal to the required expression as a function on $D$." :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ with parametrization $r(u,v)$ for $(u,v)\\in D,$ the surface integral of a scalar function $f$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}f(r(u,v))‖{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}‖\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWe first compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{u}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂u},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂u},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂u}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂u}(2 u v),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂u}(- u),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂u}(3 u - v^{2})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2 v,\\,-1,\\,3⟩ \\\\ {r}_{v}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂v},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂v}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂v}(2 u v),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂v}(- u),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂v}(3 u - v^{2})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2 u,\\,0,\\,- 2 v⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′} & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 2 v & -1 & 3 \\\\ 2 u & 0 & - 2 v\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =⟨\\begin{vmatrix}-1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & - 2 v\\end{vmatrix},\\,-\\begin{vmatrix}2 v & 3 \\\\ 2 u & - 2 v\\end{vmatrix},\\,\\begin{vmatrix}2 v & -1 \\\\ 2 u & 0\\end{vmatrix}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2 v,\\,6 u + 4 v^{2},\\,2 u⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}‖ & =‖⟨2 v,\\,6 u + 4 v^{2},\\,2 u⟩‖ \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(2 v)^{2}+(6 u + 4 v^{2})^{2}+(2 u)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{40 u^{2} + 48 u v^{2} + 16 v^{4} + 4 v^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the coordinate expressions of $r(u,v)$ into $f(x,y,z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(u,v)) & =(x - y^{2} z)\\,{|}_{x=2 u v\\,y=- u\\,z=3 u - v^{2}} \\\\  & =2 u v-1\\cdot (- u)^{2}\\cdot (3 u - v^{2}) \\\\  & =- 3 u^{3} + u^{2} v^{2} + 2 u v\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be written as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}f(r(u,v))‖{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}‖\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\,\\left(- 3 u^{3} + u^{2} v^{2} + 2 u v\\right) \\sqrt{40 u^{2} + 48 u v^{2} + 16 v^{4} + 4 v^{2}}\\,\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186560333 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2dbbf8c5-b186-50e3-83eb-a34ac7c0c068" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\left(- 2 u^{3} + 2 u^{2} v^{2} + 6 u v\\right) \\sqrt{45 u^{2} + 144 u v^{2} + 144 v^{4} + 16 v^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186560316} {:answer/id #uuid "361ee39b-048e-5ba6-8456-29b75f167d20" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\left(2 u^{2} v^{2} + 6 u v\\right) \\sqrt{45 u^{2} + 144 u v^{2} + 144 v^{4} + 16 v^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186560319} {:answer/id #uuid "3f1e9e0f-f4d1-5f9b-b01e-4c51ccc96d26" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "- 2 u^{3} + 2 u^{2} v^{2} + 6 u v" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186560321} {:answer/id #uuid "71ff934d-be68-5886-a0e6-ceea28cda382" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\left(2 u^{3} + 2 u^{2} v^{2} + 6 u v\\right) \\sqrt{45 u^{2} + 144 u v^{2} + 144 v^{4} + 16 v^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186560327} {:answer/id #uuid "f8c64255-656c-592c-8b6f-d29d8add1e50" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\left(- 2 u^{3} + 2 u^{2} v^{2} + 6 u v\\right) \\left(45 u^{2} + 144 u v^{2} + 144 v^{4} + 16 v^{2}\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186560346}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186560316} :answer-field/id #uuid "d30326ca-d748-58e4-a5fd-30f7dcf9dd69" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186560340}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "9b0c0134-9edd-51ff-b844-c0de83e1869b" :question/problem "$$\n{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}\\boxed{}\\,\\text{d}A\n$$\n\nConsider the surface integral of the function \n$f(x,y,z)=2 x - y^{2} z$ \nover the parametric surface \n\n$$\nr(u,v)=⟨3 u v,\\,- u,\\,2 u - 2 v^{2}⟩\n$$\n\nfor $(u,v)\\in D⊆{R}^{2}.$ What is the missing expression? Choose an integrand equal to the required expression as a function on $D$." :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ with parametrization $r(u,v)$ for $(u,v)\\in D,$ the surface integral of a scalar function $f$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}f(r(u,v))‖{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}‖\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWe first compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{u}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂u},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂u},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂u}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂u}(3 u v),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂u}(- u),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂u}(2 u - 2 v^{2})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨3 v,\\,-1,\\,2⟩ \\\\ {r}_{v}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂v},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂v}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂v}(3 u v),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂v}(- u),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂v}(2 u - 2 v^{2})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨3 u,\\,0,\\,- 4 v⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′} & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 3 v & -1 & 2 \\\\ 3 u & 0 & - 4 v\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =⟨\\begin{vmatrix}-1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & - 4 v\\end{vmatrix},\\,-\\begin{vmatrix}3 v & 2 \\\\ 3 u & - 4 v\\end{vmatrix},\\,\\begin{vmatrix}3 v & -1 \\\\ 3 u & 0\\end{vmatrix}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨4 v,\\,6 u + 12 v^{2},\\,3 u⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}‖ & =‖⟨4 v,\\,6 u + 12 v^{2},\\,3 u⟩‖ \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(4 v)^{2}+(6 u + 12 v^{2})^{2}+(3 u)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{45 u^{2} + 144 u v^{2} + 144 v^{4} + 16 v^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the coordinate expressions of $r(u,v)$ into $f(x,y,z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(u,v)) & =(2 x - y^{2} z)\\,{|}_{x=3 u v\\,y=- u\\,z=2 u - 2 v^{2}} \\\\  & =6 u v-1\\cdot (- u)^{2}\\cdot (2 u - 2 v^{2}) \\\\  & =- 2 u^{3} + 2 u^{2} v^{2} + 6 u v\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be written as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}f(r(u,v))‖{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}‖\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\,\\left(- 2 u^{3} + 2 u^{2} v^{2} + 6 u v\\right) \\sqrt{45 u^{2} + 144 u v^{2} + 144 v^{4} + 16 v^{2}}\\,\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186560336 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0d456bb7-3a9d-56dd-93b0-7a184a9a4649" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "- 36 u^{3} + 36 u^{2} v^{2} + 2 u v" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186560303} {:answer/id #uuid "23028f52-cd66-57e0-afed-a3ec494e3381" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\left(- 36 u^{3} + 36 u^{2} v^{2} + 2 u v\\right) \\sqrt{13 u^{2} + 16 u v^{2} + 16 v^{4} + 144 v^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186560311} {:answer/id #uuid "7194190b-9e89-5f7e-af91-ce99d3f01169" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\left(36 u^{2} v^{2} + 2 u v\\right) \\sqrt{13 u^{2} + 16 u v^{2} + 16 v^{4} + 144 v^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186560326} {:answer/id #uuid "9cc0030a-0473-5ca6-886a-e1f5a05cd61d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\left(- 36 u^{3} + 36 u^{2} v^{2} + 2 u v\\right) \\left(13 u^{2} + 16 u v^{2} + 16 v^{4} + 144 v^{2}\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186560335} {:answer/id #uuid "fe35efa1-95dd-5d26-a0cd-9af6226ea03d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\left(36 u^{3} + 36 u^{2} v^{2} + 2 u v\\right) \\sqrt{13 u^{2} + 16 u v^{2} + 16 v^{4} + 144 v^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186560348}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186560311} :answer-field/id #uuid "1f22210f-68bc-50f9-9ee4-c533602be0ab" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186560310}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "9d4bdd51-e427-51d6-919e-7d7980cc59de" :question/problem "$$\n{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}\\boxed{}\\,\\text{d}A\n$$\n\nConsider the surface integral of the function \n$f(x,y,z)=2 x - 2 y^{2} z$ \nover the parametric surface \n\n$$\nr(u,v)=⟨u v,\\,- 3 u,\\,2 u - 2 v^{2}⟩\n$$\n\nfor $(u,v)\\in D⊆{R}^{2}.$ What is the missing expression? Choose an integrand equal to the required expression as a function on $D$." :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ with parametrization $r(u,v)$ for $(u,v)\\in D,$ the surface integral of a scalar function $f$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}f(r(u,v))‖{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}‖\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWe first compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{u}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂u},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂u},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂u}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂u}(u v),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂u}(- 3 u),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂u}(2 u - 2 v^{2})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨v,\\,-3,\\,2⟩ \\\\ {r}_{v}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂v},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂v}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂v}(u v),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂v}(- 3 u),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂v}(2 u - 2 v^{2})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨u,\\,0,\\,- 4 v⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′} & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ v & -3 & 2 \\\\ u & 0 & - 4 v\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =⟨\\begin{vmatrix}-3 & 2 \\\\ 0 & - 4 v\\end{vmatrix},\\,-\\begin{vmatrix}v & 2 \\\\ u & - 4 v\\end{vmatrix},\\,\\begin{vmatrix}v & -3 \\\\ u & 0\\end{vmatrix}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨12 v,\\,2 u + 4 v^{2},\\,3 u⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}‖ & =‖⟨12 v,\\,2 u + 4 v^{2},\\,3 u⟩‖ \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(12 v)^{2}+(2 u + 4 v^{2})^{2}+(3 u)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{13 u^{2} + 16 u v^{2} + 16 v^{4} + 144 v^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the coordinate expressions of $r(u,v)$ into $f(x,y,z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(u,v)) & =(2 x - 2 y^{2} z)\\,{|}_{x=u v\\,y=- 3 u\\,z=2 u - 2 v^{2}} \\\\  & =2 u v-2\\cdot (- 3 u)^{2}\\cdot (2 u - 2 v^{2}) \\\\  & =- 36 u^{3} + 36 u^{2} v^{2} + 2 u v\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be written as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}f(r(u,v))‖{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}‖\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\,\\left(- 36 u^{3} + 36 u^{2} v^{2} + 2 u v\\right) \\sqrt{13 u^{2} + 16 u v^{2} + 16 v^{4} + 144 v^{2}}\\,\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186561104 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0f68b7fd-a05b-5ed3-be29-d3a066fa3f6d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186561103} {:answer/id #uuid "2da9c7fe-711b-5c16-9aaa-59dc3d066577" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186561107} {:answer/id #uuid "42241106-e146-5a54-bb14-23da41c8a163" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186561114} {:answer/id #uuid "7e85d298-fe5c-5353-8490-ad4ec7841c97" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186561127} {:answer/id #uuid "bbd8ac6b-ee02-58b7-80d1-363a2c5eaef1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186561140}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186561140} :answer-field/id #uuid "46f2c8c9-f5fe-5b76-ad69-56e4496a7e41" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186561115}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "264b814d-2106-5f9b-b1fe-b44e3bc8871e" :question/problem "If the path $C$ is a part of the graph $y=2 x^{2}$ traversed from the point $A(0,0)$ to point $B(1,2),$ then $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_C\\left(2 x\\right)\\,dx+\\left(2 y\\right)\\,dy=\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that our curve is given by the explicit Cartesian equation $y=2 x^{2}.$ As a result, we obtain the following parametrization of the curve:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t)=x(t)\\,i+y(t)\\,j=\\left(t\\right)\\,i+\\left(2 t^{2}\\right)\\,j,\\,t\\in [0,1]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe derivative of $r(t)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t)&=\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j=\\left(1\\right)\\,i+\\left(4 t\\right)\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now evaluate the line integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_C\\left(2 x\\right)\\,dx+\\left(2 y\\right)\\,dy &={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\left(2 x\\right)\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t+\\left(2 y\\right)\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  &={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\left(\\left(2 x\\right)\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+\\left(2 y\\right)\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\right)\\,dt \\\\  &={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\left(2 \\left(t\\right)\\right)\\cdot \\left(1\\right)+\\left(2 \\left(2 t^{2}\\right)\\right)\\cdot \\left(4 t\\right)\\,dt \\\\  &={\\int }_{0}^{1}2 t+16 t^{3}\\,dt \\\\  &={\\int }_{0}^{1}16 t^{3} + 2 t\\,dt \\\\  &=\\left[t^{2}+4 t^{4}\\right]_{0}^{1} \\\\  &=[\\left(1\\right)^{2}+4 \\left(1\\right)^{4}]-[\\left(0\\right)^{2}+4 \\left(0\\right)^{4}] \\\\  &=[1+4]-[0+0] \\\\  &=5\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186561108 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "35fb8445-5684-548d-bd47-edcfcdeafbf7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186561109} {:answer/id #uuid "41afe2ef-3ccf-5c85-b5c8-683beaddc0d3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186561113} {:answer/id #uuid "759da95f-a53d-5bde-ae51-3f153ba102f7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186561124} {:answer/id #uuid "b14b3c92-fd7d-50b3-8c75-2db22af020f1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186561137} {:answer/id #uuid "cdb11971-a604-52b5-836e-e4a5989d12c8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186561143}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186561113} :answer-field/id #uuid "6a10e453-cbdd-592f-963c-b2f496e834bc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186561122}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "2dcc6cc7-b54e-5741-ab6c-e8408719e9ef" :question/problem "If the path $C$ is a part of the graph $y=3 x^{2}$ traversed from the point $A(0,0)$ to point $B(1,3),$ then $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_C\\left(y\\right)\\,dx+\\left(3 x\\right)\\,dy=\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that our curve is given by the explicit Cartesian equation $y=3 x^{2}.$ As a result, we obtain the following parametrization of the curve:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t)=x(t)\\,i+y(t)\\,j=\\left(t\\right)\\,i+\\left(3 t^{2}\\right)\\,j,\\,t\\in [0,1]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe derivative of $r(t)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t)&=\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j=\\left(1\\right)\\,i+\\left(6 t\\right)\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now evaluate the line integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_C\\left(y\\right)\\,dx+\\left(3 x\\right)\\,dy &={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\left(y\\right)\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t+\\left(3 x\\right)\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  &={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\left(\\left(y\\right)\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+\\left(3 x\\right)\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\right)\\,dt \\\\  &={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\left(\\left(3 t^{2}\\right)\\right)\\cdot \\left(1\\right)+\\left(3 \\left(t\\right)\\right)\\cdot \\left(6 t\\right)\\,dt \\\\  &={\\int }_{0}^{1}3 t^{2}+18 t^{2}\\,dt \\\\  &={\\int }_{0}^{1}21 t^{2}\\,dt \\\\  &=\\left[7 t^{3}\\right]_{0}^{1} \\\\  &=[7 \\left(1\\right)^{3}]-[7 \\left(0\\right)^{3}] \\\\  &=[7]-[0] \\\\  &=7\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186561110 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ae0dfcc4-e263-550a-a347-77b9c856215f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-2 + 24 \\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186561135} {:answer/id #uuid "d81332bf-adb8-5841-99d3-9d72d8bfcb57" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2 - 24 \\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186561145} {:answer/id #uuid "e66cffba-5fe7-572d-8b46-9c663c477ab7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186561149} {:answer/id #uuid "e88587d0-0436-5fed-93df-1882ec786690" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "24 \\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186561150} {:answer/id #uuid "f931f17f-cdad-528e-9767-25b179cb2cab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "- 24 \\pi - 2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186561155}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186561135} :answer-field/id #uuid "3f453c7f-8458-5c6f-9e7d-b329ad3c557e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186561111}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "3c5590c4-444c-5199-8159-57617b0360a9" :question/problem "If the path $C$ is a part of the graph $y=3 \\pi x^{2}$ traversed from the point $A(0,0)$ to point $B(2,12 \\pi),$ then $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_C\\left(\\cos{\\left(\\frac{y}{x^{2}} \\right)}\\right)\\,dx+\\left(x^{2}\\right)\\,dy=\n$$ The composed integrand at the initial endpoint is interpreted by its one-sided continuous extension along $C.$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that our curve is given by the explicit Cartesian equation $y=3 \\pi x^{2}.$ As a result, we obtain the following parametrization of the curve:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t)=x(t)\\,i+y(t)\\,j=\\left(t\\right)\\,i+\\left(3 \\pi t^{2}\\right)\\,j,\\,t\\in [0,2]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe derivative of $r(t)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t)&=\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j=\\left(1\\right)\\,i+\\left(6 \\pi t\\right)\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now evaluate the line integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_C\\left(\\cos{\\left(\\frac{y}{x^{2}} \\right)}\\right)\\,dx+\\left(x^{2}\\right)\\,dy &={\\int }_{0}^{2}\\left(\\cos{\\left(\\frac{y}{x^{2}} \\right)}\\right)\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t+\\left(x^{2}\\right)\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  &={\\int }_{0}^{2}\\left(\\left(\\cos{\\left(\\frac{y}{x^{2}} \\right)}\\right)\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+\\left(x^{2}\\right)\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\right)\\,dt \\\\  &={\\int }_{0}^{2}\\left(\\cos{\\left(\\frac{\\left(3 \\pi t^{2}\\right)}{\\left(t\\right)^{2}} \\right)}\\right)\\cdot \\left(1\\right)+\\left(\\left(t\\right)^{2}\\right)\\cdot \\left(6 \\pi t\\right)\\,dt \\\\  &={\\int }_{0}^{2}-1+6 \\pi t^{3}\\,dt \\\\  &={\\int }_{0}^{2}6 \\pi t^{3} - 1\\,dt \\\\  &=\\left[- t+\\frac{3 \\pi t^{4}}{2}\\right]_{0}^{2} \\\\  &=[- \\left(2\\right)+\\frac{3 \\pi \\left(2\\right)^{4}}{2}]-[- \\left(0\\right)+\\frac{3 \\pi \\left(0\\right)^{4}}{2}] \\\\  &=[-2+24 \\pi]-[0+0] \\\\  &=-2 + 24 \\pi\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186561117 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "59cc3c27-a174-5c0d-aa04-4811c96c7884" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "- \\frac{7}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186561118} {:answer/id #uuid "a9899e7a-d7ee-5995-9047-835d19bd680a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "- \\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186561134} {:answer/id #uuid "dd99d69a-966f-5260-8310-a3c9aa6518a5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186561147} {:answer/id #uuid "e9ce83be-6fda-55b0-a2e0-508f33e9fe9d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186561151} {:answer/id #uuid "fff505a9-a05e-5262-8d36-577ee15f55fc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186561157}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186561157} :answer-field/id #uuid "4db11ed0-4b57-51fe-90ac-743b3517303e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186561116}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "578e10c6-79c2-549d-b8b1-370e0c81a999" :question/problem "If the path $C$ is a part of the graph $y=x^{2}$ traversed from the point $A(0,0)$ to point $B(1,1),$ then $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_C\\left(3 y\\right)\\,dx+\\left(2 x\\right)\\,dy=\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that our curve is given by the explicit Cartesian equation $y=x^{2}.$ As a result, we obtain the following parametrization of the curve:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t)=x(t)\\,i+y(t)\\,j=\\left(t\\right)\\,i+\\left(t^{2}\\right)\\,j,\\,t\\in [0,1]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe derivative of $r(t)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t)&=\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j=\\left(1\\right)\\,i+\\left(2 t\\right)\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now evaluate the line integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_C\\left(3 y\\right)\\,dx+\\left(2 x\\right)\\,dy &={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\left(3 y\\right)\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t+\\left(2 x\\right)\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  &={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\left(\\left(3 y\\right)\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+\\left(2 x\\right)\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\right)\\,dt \\\\  &={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\left(3 \\left(t^{2}\\right)\\right)\\cdot \\left(1\\right)+\\left(2 \\left(t\\right)\\right)\\cdot \\left(2 t\\right)\\,dt \\\\  &={\\int }_{0}^{1}3 t^{2}+4 t^{2}\\,dt \\\\  &={\\int }_{0}^{1}7 t^{2}\\,dt \\\\  &=\\left[\\frac{7 t^{3}}{3}\\right]_{0}^{1} \\\\  &=[\\frac{7 \\left(1\\right)^{3}}{3}]-[\\frac{7 \\left(0\\right)^{3}}{3}] \\\\  &=[\\frac{7}{3}]-[0] \\\\  &=\\frac{7}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186561123 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "02aa9a57-14fb-5da2-89a8-c60019da0740" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "64" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186561102} {:answer/id #uuid "2b805d00-fa7e-59e2-bf53-fb6bd5ba499b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{312}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186561106} {:answer/id #uuid "3f647e58-0307-54fb-91d2-41df1d42abcd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "- \\frac{312}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186561112} {:answer/id #uuid "779fd53c-1281-543d-924b-41becda0bec5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "- \\frac{8}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186561125} {:answer/id #uuid "93528aed-dc54-5dab-bbaa-d4d103cae7a2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "- \\frac{328}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186561129}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186561106} :answer-field/id #uuid "e58510c5-ac6e-51f9-868d-1d0c4dc0f95a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186561148}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "7162142b-dbd1-534f-9062-4b9d8572ca7b" :question/problem "If the path $C$ is a part of the graph $y=x^{2} - 3$ traversed from the point $A(0,-3)$ to point $B(2,1),$ then $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_C\\left(x^{2} y\\right)\\,dx+\\left(3 x^{2} \\left(y + 3\\right)\\right)\\,dy=\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that our curve is given by the explicit Cartesian equation $y=x^{2} - 3.$ As a result, we obtain the following parametrization of the curve:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t)=x(t)\\,i+y(t)\\,j=\\left(t\\right)\\,i+\\left(t^{2} - 3\\right)\\,j,\\,t\\in [0,2]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe derivative of $r(t)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t)&=\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j=\\left(1\\right)\\,i+\\left(2 t\\right)\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now evaluate the line integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_C\\left(x^{2} y\\right)\\,dx+\\left(3 x^{2} \\left(y + 3\\right)\\right)\\,dy &={\\int }_{0}^{2}\\left(x^{2} y\\right)\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t+\\left(3 x^{2} \\left(y + 3\\right)\\right)\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  &={\\int }_{0}^{2}\\left(\\left(x^{2} y\\right)\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+\\left(3 x^{2} \\left(y + 3\\right)\\right)\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\right)\\,dt \\\\  &={\\int }_{0}^{2}\\left(\\left(t\\right)^{2} \\left(t^{2} - 3\\right)\\right)\\cdot \\left(1\\right)+\\left(3 \\left(t\\right)^{2} \\left(\\left(t^{2} - 3\\right) + 3\\right)\\right)\\cdot \\left(2 t\\right)\\,dt \\\\  &={\\int }_{0}^{2}t^{4} - 3 t^{2}+6 t^{5}\\,dt \\\\  &={\\int }_{0}^{2}6 t^{5} + t^{4} - 3 t^{2}\\,dt \\\\  &=\\left[\\frac{t^{5}}{5}- t^{3}+t^{6}\\right]_{0}^{2} \\\\  &=[\\frac{\\left(2\\right)^{5}}{5}- \\left(2\\right)^{3}+\\left(2\\right)^{6}]-[\\frac{\\left(0\\right)^{5}}{5}- \\left(0\\right)^{3}+\\left(0\\right)^{6}] \\\\  &=[\\frac{32}{5}-8+64]-[0+0+0] \\\\  &=\\frac{312}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186561136 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "27339d99-5bd9-5b84-ada5-cae5e808d7a6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "14" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186561105} {:answer/id #uuid "8d49ffbc-81e9-5a34-8fee-73754515fc69" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-13" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186561128} {:answer/id #uuid "a4fee78c-32f3-53ad-8cfa-b12be696a948" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-14" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186561133} {:answer/id #uuid "f398ea5b-afdb-5725-b79e-fb562909bf61" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186561154} {:answer/id #uuid "ff718de6-9f5f-5ab8-baf8-a79becdbbd0f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{27}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186561156}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186561105} :answer-field/id #uuid "614c5015-0530-568c-82e0-a67fbdb96d70" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186561119}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "ae8004c0-bde5-5db3-90c2-5540e1004142" :question/problem "If the path $C$ is a part of the graph $y=3 x^{2}$ traversed from the point $A(0,0)$ to point $B(1,3),$ then $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_C\\left(x\\right)\\,dx+\\left(3 y\\right)\\,dy=\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that our curve is given by the explicit Cartesian equation $y=3 x^{2}.$ As a result, we obtain the following parametrization of the curve:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t)=x(t)\\,i+y(t)\\,j=\\left(t\\right)\\,i+\\left(3 t^{2}\\right)\\,j,\\,t\\in [0,1]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe derivative of $r(t)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t)&=\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j=\\left(1\\right)\\,i+\\left(6 t\\right)\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now evaluate the line integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_C\\left(x\\right)\\,dx+\\left(3 y\\right)\\,dy &={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\left(x\\right)\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t+\\left(3 y\\right)\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  &={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\left(\\left(x\\right)\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+\\left(3 y\\right)\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\right)\\,dt \\\\  &={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\left(\\left(t\\right)\\right)\\cdot \\left(1\\right)+\\left(3 \\left(3 t^{2}\\right)\\right)\\cdot \\left(6 t\\right)\\,dt \\\\  &={\\int }_{0}^{1}t+54 t^{3}\\,dt \\\\  &={\\int }_{0}^{1}54 t^{3} + t\\,dt \\\\  &=\\left[\\frac{t^{2}}{2}+\\frac{27 t^{4}}{2}\\right]_{0}^{1} \\\\  &=[\\frac{\\left(1\\right)^{2}}{2}+\\frac{27 \\left(1\\right)^{4}}{2}]-[\\frac{\\left(0\\right)^{2}}{2}+\\frac{27 \\left(0\\right)^{4}}{2}] \\\\  &=[\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{27}{2}]-[0+0] \\\\  &=14\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186561139 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7b51235b-d773-596c-8209-d2a82308a4fa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186561126} {:answer/id #uuid "9c858184-b694-5e2a-b80b-4d1a74942278" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186561130} {:answer/id #uuid "c3ab1e7c-29cf-5323-83d8-66e7cbc02cbc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186561142} {:answer/id #uuid "d816469b-cf8f-520a-8f3a-7253e50e1889" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186561146} {:answer/id #uuid "ef0c8b07-d501-5dbf-91af-7e7ed08f35ba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "- \\frac{5}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186561152}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186561126} :answer-field/id #uuid "cff2a487-8e9e-51dc-aacf-2e57af4a14bc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186561144}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "b69980cc-ee42-537d-9b34-61b269c46770" :question/problem "If the path $C$ is a part of the graph $y=x^{2}$ traversed from the point $A(0,0)$ to point $B(1,1),$ then $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_C\\left(3 x\\right)\\,dx+\\left(2 y\\right)\\,dy=\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that our curve is given by the explicit Cartesian equation $y=x^{2}.$ As a result, we obtain the following parametrization of the curve:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t)=x(t)\\,i+y(t)\\,j=\\left(t\\right)\\,i+\\left(t^{2}\\right)\\,j,\\,t\\in [0,1]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe derivative of $r(t)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t)&=\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j=\\left(1\\right)\\,i+\\left(2 t\\right)\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now evaluate the line integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_C\\left(3 x\\right)\\,dx+\\left(2 y\\right)\\,dy &={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\left(3 x\\right)\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t+\\left(2 y\\right)\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  &={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\left(\\left(3 x\\right)\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+\\left(2 y\\right)\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\right)\\,dt \\\\  &={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\left(3 \\left(t\\right)\\right)\\cdot \\left(1\\right)+\\left(2 \\left(t^{2}\\right)\\right)\\cdot \\left(2 t\\right)\\,dt \\\\  &={\\int }_{0}^{1}3 t+4 t^{3}\\,dt \\\\  &={\\int }_{0}^{1}4 t^{3} + 3 t\\,dt \\\\  &=\\left[\\frac{3 t^{2}}{2}+t^{4}\\right]_{0}^{1} \\\\  &=[\\frac{3 \\left(1\\right)^{2}}{2}+\\left(1\\right)^{4}]-[\\frac{3 \\left(0\\right)^{2}}{2}+\\left(0\\right)^{4}] \\\\  &=[\\frac{3}{2}+1]-[0+0] \\\\  &=\\frac{5}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186561153 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "688533d7-da94-5d62-886b-1a90ca349e53" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-2 + 52 \\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186561120} {:answer/id #uuid "69684ec4-4be0-5868-aff3-3fed587c9d86" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "52 \\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186561121} {:answer/id #uuid "a0e57cc0-e23e-5364-8631-e13e2e412ba6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "- 52 \\pi - 2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186561131} {:answer/id #uuid "b55dff06-4093-5a7d-8247-97e5471839db" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186561138} {:answer/id #uuid "c1ed43f6-4855-52a9-a384-1d599ac85543" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2 - 52 \\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186561141}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186561120} :answer-field/id #uuid "a297ede3-80df-5c9e-a44a-147e80cb4715" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186561132}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "f3771e2f-30a0-504d-a491-225f9ef42173" :question/problem "If the path $C$ is a part of the graph $y=3 \\pi x^{2}$ traversed from the point $A(1,3 \\pi)$ to point $B(3,27 \\pi),$ then $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_C\\left(\\sin{\\left(\\frac{y}{2 x^{2}} \\right)}\\right)\\,dx+\\left(x\\right)\\,dy=\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that our curve is given by the explicit Cartesian equation $y=3 \\pi x^{2}.$ As a result, we obtain the following parametrization of the curve:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t)=x(t)\\,i+y(t)\\,j=\\left(t\\right)\\,i+\\left(3 \\pi t^{2}\\right)\\,j,\\,t\\in [1,3]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe derivative of $r(t)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t)&=\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j=\\left(1\\right)\\,i+\\left(6 \\pi t\\right)\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now evaluate the line integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_C\\left(\\sin{\\left(\\frac{y}{2 x^{2}} \\right)}\\right)\\,dx+\\left(x\\right)\\,dy &={\\int }_{1}^{3}\\left(\\sin{\\left(\\frac{y}{2 x^{2}} \\right)}\\right)\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t+\\left(x\\right)\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  &={\\int }_{1}^{3}\\left(\\left(\\sin{\\left(\\frac{y}{2 x^{2}} \\right)}\\right)\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+\\left(x\\right)\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\right)\\,dt \\\\  &={\\int }_{1}^{3}\\left(\\sin{\\left(\\frac{\\left(3 \\pi t^{2}\\right)}{2 \\left(t\\right)^{2}} \\right)}\\right)\\cdot \\left(1\\right)+\\left(\\left(t\\right)\\right)\\cdot \\left(6 \\pi t\\right)\\,dt \\\\  &={\\int }_{1}^{3}-1+6 \\pi t^{2}\\,dt \\\\  &={\\int }_{1}^{3}6 \\pi t^{2} - 1\\,dt \\\\  &=\\left[- t+2 \\pi t^{3}\\right]_{1}^{3} \\\\  &=[- \\left(3\\right)+2 \\pi \\left(3\\right)^{3}]-[- \\left(1\\right)+2 \\pi \\left(1\\right)^{3}] \\\\  &=[-3+54 \\pi]-[-1+2 \\pi] \\\\  &=-2 + 52 \\pi\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186561161 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4e0e0a84-34b9-5bd4-ad76-0ee82146d7a1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x=9\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=\\frac{13}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186561166} {:answer/id #uuid "8e0d75fc-7815-575e-a88d-f7f2e8f57710" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x=9\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=- \\frac{13}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186561174} {:answer/id #uuid "900783cc-81ae-5b41-afd7-f063ff81df7f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x=\\frac{13}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=9\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186561175} {:answer/id #uuid "be24c75c-58c5-5557-959d-739eb0ef8bac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x=9\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=\\frac{13}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186561181} {:answer/id #uuid "ffb5ac79-05df-54e4-ba6a-a76300856560" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x=81\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=\\frac{169}{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186561190}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186561181} :answer-field/id #uuid "3bf5092d-a34a-5701-becf-a9d5bd069031" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186561165}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "05dcfa30-0b39-5cca-b439-94f282b458a7" :question/problem "![](images/05/0567f92e27f3a9699065515bd8230ac21154de2790d08433de65d4d783187b78.png)\n\nThe axes have half-unit ticks, with even integers labeled on the grid margins. The ellipse is centered at the origin. Which of the following sets of parametric equations, defined for $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ),$ traverse the ellipse once in the counterclockwise direction starting at the marked point $A$?" :question/worked-solution "The parametric equations of an ellipse centered at the origin with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ are\n\n$$\nx=a\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=b\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nUnder this parametrization, the ellipse is traversed once in the counterclockwise direction, starting from $(a,0).$\n\nIn our case, $a=9$ and $b=\\frac{13}{2}.$ Therefore, the parametric equations of the ellipse are\n\n$$\nx=9\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=\\frac{13}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186561163 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "67c8c9be-a203-59f1-80b3-6bdf573103dc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "9\\operatorname{cos}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186561169}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186561169} :answer-field/id #uuid "5f4b3dd5-b501-5067-9912-25a9f62b94f6" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186561167} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c26781a5-d15d-5940-9cd8-d0e8375698ad" :a